WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.335 --> 00:00:08.205
One of the most important lessons
that I had to learn in my life

00:00:08.205 --> 00:00:11.008
is that when it comes
to our growth and development,

00:00:11.042 --> 00:00:14.912
there is no substitute
for a tough piece of feedback

00:00:14.912 --> 00:00:17.314
from someone who cares about us.

00:00:17.348 --> 00:00:20.317
And when we can make it safe enough
for the people in our lives

00:00:20.351 --> 00:00:21.952
to tell us those hard things,

00:00:21.986 --> 00:00:24.055
it not only benefits us,

00:00:24.088 --> 00:00:26.891
it can spark transformation
in our workplaces

00:00:26.924 --> 00:00:28.492
and in our families.

00:00:29.026 --> 00:00:31.896
And I learned this lesson
the way that I learned most things,

00:00:31.896 --> 00:00:33.097
which is the hard way,

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.933
and it was when I got
a tough piece of feedback

00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:38.135
from my best friend at work, Christy.

00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:41.072
So I met Christy years ago

00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:44.341
when I left the only organization
I had ever worked for

00:00:44.341 --> 00:00:47.211
to start over at a new company,

00:00:47.244 --> 00:00:48.612
and I was freaking out.

00:00:48.612 --> 00:00:54.618
And Christy was this incredibly
kind and welcoming person

00:00:54.618 --> 00:00:58.522
who just really went out of her way
to help me be successful.

00:00:58.556 --> 00:01:00.391
And we became fast friends.

00:01:00.724 --> 00:01:03.094
And then one day we got into an argument.

00:01:03.561 --> 00:01:04.795
Not uncommon for us --

00:01:04.829 --> 00:01:07.798
we’re both pretty stubborn
and opinionated people --

00:01:07.832 --> 00:01:11.302
and we resolved it,
and I thought everything was fine.

00:01:12.303 --> 00:01:14.071
She had a very different reaction,

00:01:14.105 --> 00:01:15.606
and the next day she came in

00:01:15.639 --> 00:01:18.275
and she gave me this feedback,
and I'll never forget it.

00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:19.944
She said,

00:01:19.944 --> 00:01:24.115
"It just seemed like while we were arguing

00:01:24.148 --> 00:01:26.484
that you needed me to feel stupid.”

00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:30.888
And if you've ever had somebody
make you feel that way,

00:01:30.888 --> 00:01:32.256
you know it’s awful.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.992
So here's my best friend at work,

00:01:35.025 --> 00:01:36.427
somebody that I care about,

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.195
she's told me that I've hurt her,

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:41.565
and so of course, my immediate reaction

00:01:41.565 --> 00:01:42.867
is I get very defensive.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.135
So I remember I was like,

00:01:44.168 --> 00:01:46.337
running through all of this
in my head, right?

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.739
Like, "How could you say that?

00:01:48.772 --> 00:01:50.174
That's completely unfair.

00:01:50.207 --> 00:01:52.143
I would never think that about you."

00:01:52.176 --> 00:01:55.779
And then I was just listing off
all the reasons that she was wrong.

00:01:55.813 --> 00:01:58.315
(Laughter)

00:01:59.683 --> 00:02:01.318
Now, thankfully,

00:02:01.352 --> 00:02:03.354
I didn't say any of that out loud.

00:02:03.387 --> 00:02:04.588
And --

00:02:04.588 --> 00:02:06.123
(Laughter)

00:02:06.157 --> 00:02:08.459
All I could think to say
in the moment was,

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:10.127
"Thank you for telling me."

00:02:10.161 --> 00:02:13.631
And I meant it because she could have done
so many other things, right?

00:02:13.664 --> 00:02:16.800
She could have gossiped
about me behind my back,

00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:19.803
she could have gone to our boss
and complained about me,

00:02:19.837 --> 00:02:22.273
she could have done
what I probably would have done,

00:02:22.273 --> 00:02:25.142
which is just silently resent me
and not said anything.

00:02:25.176 --> 00:02:26.377
(Laughter)

00:02:26.377 --> 00:02:28.646
But she had the courage
to come and talk to me.

00:02:29.647 --> 00:02:32.716
And the truth is,
I've gotten this feedback before.

00:02:33.717 --> 00:02:36.420
And the feedback is
that when I'm not at my best,

00:02:36.453 --> 00:02:38.455
I come across as arrogant,

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:42.493
condescending, self-righteous
and a know-it-all --

00:02:42.493 --> 00:02:44.428
which is a really nice combination

00:02:44.461 --> 00:02:47.198
and a great way to make friends,
if you're interested.

00:02:47.932 --> 00:02:49.700
(Laughter)

00:02:50.834 --> 00:02:53.337
It's the thing in my life
I am most embarrassed about

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.005
and oftentimes ashamed of.

00:02:55.873 --> 00:02:57.975
And so I'm telling this
to Christy and I say,

00:02:57.975 --> 00:02:59.577
“I know I do this, and I’m sorry.

00:02:59.577 --> 00:03:01.512
I don't want to,

00:03:01.545 --> 00:03:03.981
but I can't hear myself
when I'm doing it.

00:03:03.981 --> 00:03:05.416
Can you help me?"

00:03:05.449 --> 00:03:08.452
And she says, "Sure, I'll just tell you
when you're doing it."

00:03:09.053 --> 00:03:14.258
Not like that, I meant,
“Do you have a book I could read or like,

00:03:14.291 --> 00:03:16.727
I don't know, like a training
or something like that

00:03:16.727 --> 00:03:18.729
that I could take, you know, to fix this?”

00:03:18.729 --> 00:03:20.531
And she said, "Well, how about this?

00:03:20.531 --> 00:03:23.067
I'll just say 'You're doing your thing.'"

00:03:24.401 --> 00:03:25.603
Worth a shot.

00:03:25.636 --> 00:03:28.639
So anytime we'd be talking
and I would start a sentence with,

00:03:28.672 --> 00:03:31.075
"Well, actually," or something like that,

00:03:31.108 --> 00:03:34.245
she would just, "Hey, Chris,
you're doing your thing."

00:03:34.778 --> 00:03:36.547
And it was so helpful.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.215
I needed the feedback.

00:03:38.249 --> 00:03:39.483
I could start to hear it

00:03:39.516 --> 00:03:41.685
and so I could start to improve on it.

00:03:41.685 --> 00:03:44.722
[A] little embarrassed to find out
how often I do it,

00:03:44.755 --> 00:03:48.559
but I did start to get better,

00:03:48.559 --> 00:03:52.229
and I noticed the transformation
of my relationships almost immediately.

00:03:52.763 --> 00:03:55.232
My wife would later tell me
that this whole process,

00:03:55.266 --> 00:03:57.301
it made it easier for her to talk to me,

00:03:57.334 --> 00:03:59.336
and that allowed us to have conversations

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.072
that we hadn't been able to have
up until that point.

00:04:02.506 --> 00:04:03.941
So I'm growing,

00:04:03.974 --> 00:04:06.010
my relationships are transforming.

00:04:06.644 --> 00:04:10.781
And then it started to inspire
other people to do the same thing.

00:04:10.781 --> 00:04:12.283
And it started with Christy.

00:04:12.316 --> 00:04:13.584
She came to me one day,

00:04:13.617 --> 00:04:14.818
"What's my thing?"

00:04:14.852 --> 00:04:16.654
(Laughter)

00:04:16.687 --> 00:04:18.989
And I kind of think
we all have a thing, right?

00:04:19.023 --> 00:04:21.558
Like something that we do
that we're not at our best,

00:04:21.592 --> 00:04:23.093
gets in our way.

00:04:23.127 --> 00:04:24.395
And so Christy's thing,

00:04:24.395 --> 00:04:26.530
and I have her permission to share this,

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:29.166
is -- just be clear --

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:31.735
is that when she gets upset
and she's not at her best,

00:04:31.735 --> 00:04:34.905
she can go from zero
to 100 [in] about two seconds,

00:04:34.905 --> 00:04:36.740
and she'll say something she regrets.

00:04:36.774 --> 00:04:38.409
OK, so she says,

00:04:38.442 --> 00:04:40.244
“Yeah, that’s it.

00:04:40.277 --> 00:04:42.513
And you're going to tell me
when I'm doing it."

00:04:42.546 --> 00:04:44.081
No, no.

00:04:44.081 --> 00:04:45.382
(Laughter)

00:04:45.416 --> 00:04:47.217
Absolutely not.

00:04:47.217 --> 00:04:48.385
Are you serious?

00:04:48.419 --> 00:04:49.787
"I've been doing it for you,

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:51.555
you have to do it for me.”

00:04:51.588 --> 00:04:52.723
Which is fair.

00:04:52.756 --> 00:04:55.726
So first time I had to tell her

00:04:55.726 --> 00:04:56.894
I'm terrified.

00:04:56.927 --> 00:04:58.062
Remember, she's upset.

00:04:58.095 --> 00:05:01.131
She's up here and I'm like,

00:05:01.165 --> 00:05:02.333
"Hey, buddy ..."

00:05:02.366 --> 00:05:04.735
(Laughter)

00:05:04.735 --> 00:05:08.105
"I think you might be doing your thing."

00:05:09.273 --> 00:05:12.142
And she instantly cools off
and she looks at me and she says,

00:05:12.176 --> 00:05:13.444
"Oh my gosh, you're right.

00:05:13.444 --> 00:05:14.645
Thank you."

00:05:15.112 --> 00:05:17.014
And then she started to improve.

00:05:17.047 --> 00:05:19.983
And then I felt safer to keep
giving her that feedback.

00:05:20.017 --> 00:05:23.087
And both of us are like,
we might be on to something here.

00:05:24.188 --> 00:05:26.590
Then there’s a restructure
in our department,

00:05:26.623 --> 00:05:28.225
I become Christy's supervisor.

00:05:31.562 --> 00:05:33.731
And one of the first things
she says to me is,

00:05:33.731 --> 00:05:36.900
"I'm just bummed I can't do
that feedback thing with you anymore."

00:05:36.934 --> 00:05:38.102
"Why?"

00:05:38.135 --> 00:05:40.604
"You're my boss,
I can't talk to you like that."

00:05:40.637 --> 00:05:42.606
"No, I need you to talk to me like that."

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:45.642
And this is key because
it's not just our own defensiveness

00:05:45.676 --> 00:05:48.645
that gets in the way of people
giving us the feedback.

00:05:48.645 --> 00:05:51.715
There's these other barriers,
like titles and roles,

00:05:51.715 --> 00:05:53.917
that can make it feel scary to talk to us.

00:05:53.951 --> 00:05:57.087
I imagine you're probably not going to
walk into your CEO's office

00:05:57.087 --> 00:05:58.255
or something like that,

00:05:58.288 --> 00:06:01.592
"I have some thoughts on your performance
I'd like to share with you."

00:06:01.625 --> 00:06:03.660
Like, you're probably
not going to do that.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.028
I did have to do that once.

00:06:05.028 --> 00:06:07.030
I had to give my CEO critical feedback,

00:06:07.064 --> 00:06:08.732
and she gave me a hug.

00:06:09.533 --> 00:06:13.404
And for just a second, I thought
it was like one of those mafia-style hugs.

00:06:13.404 --> 00:06:15.906
Like, "That was the biggest
mistake you ever made,"

00:06:15.939 --> 00:06:17.274
or something like that.

00:06:17.307 --> 00:06:18.509
(Laughter)

00:06:18.542 --> 00:06:20.377
But it was genuine

00:06:20.411 --> 00:06:22.613
and it made me so proud to work for her.

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:24.882
So I'm telling this to Christy,

00:06:24.882 --> 00:06:26.950
"You've got to keep
giving me the feedback."

00:06:26.984 --> 00:06:28.585
And that has not been a problem.

00:06:28.585 --> 00:06:29.753
(Laughter)

00:06:29.787 --> 00:06:32.856
But then it started to inspire
other people to do the same thing.

00:06:32.856 --> 00:06:35.325
So I've got this new team
and they all come to me,

00:06:35.359 --> 00:06:37.461
"We want to know what our thing is."

00:06:37.494 --> 00:06:39.163
I've got managers in other areas,

00:06:39.196 --> 00:06:41.565
"How do we make this happen on our teams?"

00:06:42.299 --> 00:06:44.168
But where this really broke open for me

00:06:44.201 --> 00:06:46.336
was one day when I was
talking to my sister,

00:06:46.336 --> 00:06:47.971
and I was telling her this story

00:06:48.005 --> 00:06:50.207
and how I'm like, "Hey,
somebody at work said

00:06:50.207 --> 00:06:53.110
I can come across
as arrogant and condescending.

00:06:53.143 --> 00:06:54.411
Can you believe that?"

00:06:54.445 --> 00:06:57.014
(Laughter)

00:06:57.047 --> 00:06:59.450
And she paused

00:06:59.450 --> 00:07:01.151
And she said, "Yeah."

00:07:02.886 --> 00:07:04.755
And I was completely blindsided,

00:07:04.788 --> 00:07:06.723
which doesn't make any sense, of course,

00:07:06.757 --> 00:07:09.159
because she would have been
one of the first people

00:07:09.193 --> 00:07:10.594
to have to deal with this.

00:07:10.627 --> 00:07:13.197
And it turns out it had bothered
her for a long time.

00:07:13.931 --> 00:07:15.866
But she hadn't talked to me about it.

00:07:17.034 --> 00:07:20.204
And she told me

00:07:20.237 --> 00:07:24.174
"There were times in my life
that I really needed you,

00:07:24.208 --> 00:07:25.576
but I didn't call

00:07:25.609 --> 00:07:28.312
because I just didn't want
to hear that tone."

00:07:30.047 --> 00:07:31.949
And that was so painful to hear.

00:07:34.251 --> 00:07:36.854
And it was the breakthrough moment
in our relationship.

00:07:36.854 --> 00:07:40.524
Because now when she has a problem
with me, she can just tell me.

00:07:40.557 --> 00:07:42.626
And that has meant everything to us.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.430
And after that, I was like,
I've got to get feedback from everyone.

00:07:46.463 --> 00:07:49.700
So I do this with my boss,
with my teammates,

00:07:49.733 --> 00:07:51.768
I do this with my wife and son.

00:07:51.768 --> 00:07:54.738
My son will say to me,
"Dad, can I give you some feedback?"

00:07:55.672 --> 00:07:57.374
I love it, I love it.

00:07:58.242 --> 00:08:00.010
Usually, yeah.

00:08:00.043 --> 00:08:02.212
(Laughter)

00:08:02.913 --> 00:08:04.948
I don't always agree with it,

00:08:04.982 --> 00:08:07.518
but I'm such a better dad because of it.

00:08:07.551 --> 00:08:09.119
And more important than that

00:08:09.152 --> 00:08:10.754
is that it means everything to me

00:08:10.787 --> 00:08:12.890
that he feels safe enough
to say that to me.

00:08:13.824 --> 00:08:15.425
And I've also had the opportunity

00:08:15.459 --> 00:08:18.228
to help hundreds of other people
create similar experiences

00:08:18.262 --> 00:08:19.796
in their own relationships.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.299
Most of us, we want
to have these important

00:08:22.332 --> 00:08:24.334
and difficult conversations,

00:08:24.368 --> 00:08:27.204
but we put too much pressure
on the Christies of the world

00:08:27.204 --> 00:08:29.206
to show up again and again

00:08:29.239 --> 00:08:31.408
with the courage to say the hard thing.

00:08:31.408 --> 00:08:32.743
And that's a mistake.

00:08:33.176 --> 00:08:36.146
There's too many barriers
to that happening consistently.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:38.148
As feedback receivers,

00:08:38.181 --> 00:08:40.050
we have to make it safer,

00:08:40.083 --> 00:08:42.152
and we have to lower the amount of courage

00:08:42.185 --> 00:08:44.621
that it requires to have
that conversation.

00:08:45.489 --> 00:08:47.491
Now, thankfully, this is a skill

00:08:47.524 --> 00:08:50.160
that we can learn
and that we can practice.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.696
And if you want to, you can use
an approach that I use

00:08:52.729 --> 00:08:54.665
called receiving feedback with tact.

00:08:55.365 --> 00:08:56.767
So if you want to try this,

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.836
pick someone in your life that you want
some more feedback from,

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.271
someone who cares about you,

00:09:01.271 --> 00:09:04.575
but they'll be honest with you
if you make it a little bit safer.

00:09:04.575 --> 00:09:07.144
Then don't wait
for them to give you feedback,

00:09:07.177 --> 00:09:08.612
go out and ask for it.

00:09:08.946 --> 00:09:11.348
Just don't make the mistake
that I made early on,

00:09:11.348 --> 00:09:13.917
I was working with this group
of managers and I said,

00:09:13.951 --> 00:09:16.720
"Go out and get some feedback
from some of your employees."

00:09:16.753 --> 00:09:20.090
And they're like, "OK,
how am I doing as your manager?"

00:09:20.691 --> 00:09:22.392
And everyone was like, "Great!"

00:09:22.426 --> 00:09:23.660
(Laughter)

00:09:23.694 --> 00:09:24.861
"Why?"

00:09:24.895 --> 00:09:25.896
(Laughter)

00:09:25.929 --> 00:09:27.064
It's too scary, right?

00:09:27.097 --> 00:09:29.533
We need to narrow that question.

00:09:29.566 --> 00:09:31.001
So I might say,

00:09:31.034 --> 00:09:33.470
"I want to get better
at running our staff meetings.

00:09:33.503 --> 00:09:36.873
What's one piece of advice you have for me
on how I could improve?"

00:09:37.541 --> 00:09:38.709
And then here's the key.

00:09:38.742 --> 00:09:40.310
It's how you respond.

00:09:40.344 --> 00:09:43.347
That's going to be the difference
between you learning something

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:45.115
and building trust with this person,

00:09:45.115 --> 00:09:46.316
or the opposite.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:49.252
So when they give you the feedback
that you asked for,

00:09:49.252 --> 00:09:51.388
you can respond with tact.

00:09:51.421 --> 00:09:53.857
So that stands for thankfulness,

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:55.225
acknowledgment,

00:09:55.225 --> 00:09:56.426
commitment

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:59.162
and then we'll end where we began,
a second thankfulness.

00:09:59.196 --> 00:10:00.964
We'll take a brief look at each one.

00:10:00.964 --> 00:10:02.332
So that first thankfulness,

00:10:02.332 --> 00:10:05.369
you're trying to calm down
that initial fight-or-flight response

00:10:05.402 --> 00:10:07.471
that you might both be experiencing.

00:10:07.504 --> 00:10:10.273
So I might say "That wasn't easy to hear,

00:10:10.307 --> 00:10:12.843
but I'm really glad
you were willing to say it."

00:10:12.876 --> 00:10:14.077
With acknowledgment,

00:10:14.077 --> 00:10:17.447
you want to make the other person
feel seen and heard first,

00:10:17.481 --> 00:10:20.450
because that's your entryway
into an authentic dialogue.

00:10:20.484 --> 00:10:22.653
They're expecting you to defend yourself,

00:10:22.686 --> 00:10:25.856
and all that's going to do is
it's just going to shut them down

00:10:25.889 --> 00:10:27.658
or gear them up for a fight.

00:10:27.691 --> 00:10:29.292
You don't have to agree.

00:10:29.292 --> 00:10:30.861
Just acknowledge what they said,

00:10:30.861 --> 00:10:33.563
acknowledge the feelings
underneath what they said,

00:10:33.597 --> 00:10:35.866
and then you can have a real conversation.

00:10:35.899 --> 00:10:38.869
And that will eventually lead you
to some kind of a commitment.

00:10:38.869 --> 00:10:40.437
If you agree with the feedback,

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:42.973
it could be the behavior change
you're going to make.

00:10:43.006 --> 00:10:44.841
If you don't agree or you're not sure,

00:10:44.841 --> 00:10:47.778
it could be to gather a few
more perspectives and circle back.

00:10:47.778 --> 00:10:50.981
It could be that you need a little
bit of time and space to process,

00:10:51.014 --> 00:10:54.117
but you're going to get back to them
at a specific date and time.

00:10:54.117 --> 00:10:56.253
Just be clear what's next
and follow through.

00:10:56.286 --> 00:10:58.488
And the second thankfulness
is my favorite part

00:10:58.488 --> 00:11:00.891
because it's so easy to do
and it's very powerful.

00:11:00.924 --> 00:11:04.161
What you're trying to do here is reinforce
and reward the behavior

00:11:04.194 --> 00:11:06.329
so that they keep doing it.

00:11:06.363 --> 00:11:10.100
Most of us don't do things
that are painful or unpleasant,

00:11:10.133 --> 00:11:12.135
like giving feedback,

00:11:12.169 --> 00:11:14.705
unless there's some sort
of a benefit for doing it.

00:11:14.705 --> 00:11:17.340
So when the conversation's over,
send them a quick text.

00:11:17.374 --> 00:11:18.542
"Thank you."

00:11:18.575 --> 00:11:21.878
Or do what one of my colleagues did
when I gave him tough feedback,

00:11:21.912 --> 00:11:24.081
he wrote me a handwritten thank-you card.

00:11:24.648 --> 00:11:29.019
Just do something that makes it feel
like it was worth the risk to tell you.

00:11:30.087 --> 00:11:33.423
So it's thankfulness,
acknowledgment, commitment,

00:11:33.423 --> 00:11:35.125
and then a second thankfulness.

00:11:35.459 --> 00:11:36.660
Do this consistently

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:38.862
and you're going to be amazed
at what you learn

00:11:38.895 --> 00:11:41.865
and the ways that it starts
to benefit your relationships.

00:11:41.865 --> 00:11:45.435
There is likely someone
in your life who cares about you

00:11:45.435 --> 00:11:49.239
and has something to tell you
that you might not want to hear,

00:11:49.272 --> 00:11:51.341
but it could change your life if you did.

00:11:51.374 --> 00:11:53.443
If you want the benefits of that feedback,

00:11:53.443 --> 00:11:55.912
ask for it and then respond with tact.

00:11:56.313 --> 00:12:00.117
I have seen it do wonders
for so many people, including me.

00:12:01.118 --> 00:12:02.519
As I was preparing for today,

00:12:02.552 --> 00:12:05.689
I shared a draft with my sister
and this is what she told me.

00:12:05.689 --> 00:12:07.057
She said,

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:10.627
"Chris, you've completely flipped
the way that you talk to me,

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:13.630
and I can't even remember
what you used to sound like.

00:12:14.331 --> 00:12:18.235
I can be completely myself around you,
even though we're so different."

00:12:19.102 --> 00:12:21.238
So go out and get
a tough piece of feedback

00:12:21.238 --> 00:12:23.140
from someone who cares about you

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:24.508
and let it change your life.

00:12:24.541 --> 00:12:25.675
Thank you.

00:12:25.709 --> 00:12:28.812
(Cheers and applause)

